Machine Coding Round: Task Management System

Duration: 90 minutes | Language: any | Storage: in-memory only (no DB, UI or framework)

Context

You are building the backend of an internal task tracker for a small engineering team. Users create tasks, assign them, move them through a workflow, and look them up in different ways. A driver (main or tests) is enough for input and output. No REST layer is needed.

Functional requirements

1. Users

Register a user with an id and a name.
Look up a user by id.

2. Tasks

Fields: id, title, description, creator, assignee (optional at creation), priority, due date, status, labels, created and updated timestamps.
Priority: LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, CRITICAL.
Status: TODO, IN_PROGRESS, IN_REVIEW, DONE. A new task starts in TODO.
Operations: create, delete, update (title, description, priority, due date, labels), assign or reassign.
The due date at creation cannot be in the past.

3. Status workflow

Allowed transitions:
TODO → IN_PROGRESS
IN_PROGRESS → IN_REVIEW
IN_REVIEW → IN_PROGRESS
IN_REVIEW → DONE
Every other transition is rejected with a clear error.
A task must have an assignee before it can leave TODO.

4. Subtasks

A task can have subtasks, one level deep only (a subtask cannot have subtasks).
A parent cannot move to DONE until all its subtasks are DONE.
Deleting a parent deletes its subtasks.

5. Search and listing

Filter by any combination of assignee, status, priority and label.
Sort by due date, priority or creation time, ascending or descending.
List overdue tasks (past due date and not DONE).

6. Activity history

Record every change: creation, assignment, status change and field updates.
Each entry holds who made the change, the field, the old value, the new value and the timestamp.
Fetch a task’s history in chronological order.

7. Notifications

The assignee is notified when a task is assigned to them or when its status changes.
The creator is notified when the task reaches DONE.
Printing to the console is enough for delivery.
Non-functional requirements
Concurrency: several users may update the same task at the same time. Simultaneous status changes or reassignments must never corrupt state or allow an illegal transition.
Extensibility: a new status, transition rule, filter, sort criterion or notification channel should need minimal changes to existing code.
Validation: invalid input (unknown user or task, illegal transition, past due date) must produce a meaningful error, never a silent failure.
Performance: lookup by task id must be O(1). For filtered listing a scan is acceptable, but be ready to say how you would speed it up.
Code quality: clear separation of responsibilities, readable naming, no business logic in the driver, no global mutable state.
Sample scenario
registerUser(u1, "Asha")
registerUser(u2, "Ravi")

createTask(by=u1, title="Fix login bug", priority=HIGH, due=2026-11-05)
  -> T1 created (TODO, unassigned)

changeStatus(T1, IN_PROGRESS, by=u1)
  -> ERROR: T1 has no assignee

assign(T1, u2, by=u1)
  -> [Notify u2] T1 assigned to you

changeStatus(T1, IN_PROGRESS, by=u2)
  -> [Notify u2] T1: TODO -> IN_PROGRESS

changeStatus(T1, DONE, by=u2)
  -> ERROR: Invalid transition IN_PROGRESS -> DONE

addSubtask(T1, title="Write tests")
  -> T1.1 created (TODO)

changeStatus(T1, IN_REVIEW, by=u2)
  -> [Notify u2] T1: IN_PROGRESS -> IN_REVIEW

changeStatus(T1, DONE, by=u2)
  -> ERROR: T1 has incomplete subtasks (T1.1)

listTasks(assignee=u2, status=IN_REVIEW, sortBy=DUE_DATE, order=ASC)
  -> [T1]

getHistory(T1)
  -> created by u1 | assignee: null -> u2 by u1 | status: TODO -> IN_PROGRESS by u2 | status: IN_PROGRESS -> IN_REVIEW by u2
Deliverables
Working, runnable code.
A driver that demonstrates:
the happy path
an invalid transition being rejected
the subtask-completion rule
filtering and sorting
history output
one concurrent-update scenario
A short explanation of your design choices and what you would add next.
Out of scope

Authentication, persistence, REST APIs, recurring tasks, comments and attachments.

How it is evaluated
Does it run and cover the requirements correctly?
Is the code readable, modular and easy to extend?
Is it correct under concurrent access?
Are edge cases and errors handled?
Does the demo cover the key scenarios?